Infoblox is investigating a supply-chain breach in which attackers allegedly used a vendor’s access to steal employee and customer dataExposed information reportedly includes employee personal details and customer network configuration backupsCompany is offering identity protection services and warning affected individuals about phishing and fraud risksInfoblox has said it is currently investigating reports of a cyberattack and sensitive data theft. Initial reports describe it as a third-party supply chain attack in which employee data and customer network information were exfiltrated. The Register revealed how Infoblox worked with a third-party data migration vendor which was granted trusted access to its SharePoint system, meaning the unidentified attackers apparently obtained login credentials for vendor accounts, used them to access Infoblox’s Microsoft SharePoint instances, and pulled data straight from there.Latest Videos FromTechRadar Watch full video here: NIOS deployments and personal dataExactly who the attackers are, or how they obtained the credentials, was not explained. Usually, they either steal the passwords with infostealers, or trick victims into handing over the credentials themselves. The former method is easy in theory - get the victim to download malware and the program sends over stored passwords, authentication cookies, sensitive documents, and other files. The latter is somewhat more difficult since it involves creating a fake login page of a known - and used - online service that secretly relays all submitted information, sending it to the victim, and hoping they try to log in. This is usually done through phishing emails that warn the victim they will lose access to accounts, or that the affiliated data will be deleted, making them hurry and not pay attention to what they’re doing.Among the nabbed information are some customers’ Network Identity Operating System (NIOS) deployments, The Register claims. “These are saved snapshots of customers’ network data, such as DNS, DHCP configurations, Grid databases, and reporting logs, that can be used to fully recover the appliance or grid in case of a system failure or faulty upgrade.”
